ravi singh is the head of the australian  sikh association he represents a faith  that's considered a minority religion in  australia but is the fastest growing he  says despite its rising popularity it's  still widely misunderstood in the  country and is often vilified by some  members of the public  sikhs look different  to a normal person veering turban than  sporting beards  so  in a way it's a  it's a  very difficult situation for for growing  kids especially in the in the schools  legislation proposed by australia's  parliament would have offered protection  to people who felt exposed because of  their own religious beliefs and such  discrimination in their workplace school  or university would have been banned but  critics argued the bill would enable  prejudice against other groups including  gay teachers and students and should  cover all forms of discrimination  it was amended to include laws  preventing religious schools  discriminating against students on  grounds of their gender and sexuality  that caused division in federal  parliament and the bill was shelved  politics should be left out and it  should be all about people who they  serve  so at the end of the day everybody  should be treated equal  some community leaders say it's not just  a matter of discrimination but also of  safety  since the massacre of 51 people by a  gunman in an attack on two mosques in  christchurch three years ago  members of some religious groups say  they feel targeted and this bill offered  greater protection  women are concerned about wearing the  veil and stepping outside because of  the attention that attracts the  vilification etc and certainly we've had  direct reports where women for instance  think twice before they step out  many in australia consider the country  promotes a multicultural environment but  there are no national laws specifically  protecting people against religious  discrimination  if they're raising concern they feel  that their rights are being undermined  their religious rights and it's  something we should take seriously the  religious discrimination bill may be  shelved for now though some faiths are  hoping that federal parliament will  revive it but given the contentious  nature of the debate that's unlikely to  happen anytime soon and a federal  election is expected by may this year  sarah clark al jazeera brisbane  australia
 

